To create a text editor that includes multiple lists (such as unordered and ordered lists) using Angular, you can follow these steps:

1. **Set up your Angular project**: If you haven't already, set up an Angular project.

2. **Create the basic component**: Start by creating a new Angular component for your text editor.

3. **HTML Template**:
   - Use HTML template syntax to define the structure of your text editor.
   - Implement multi-selection support for list items.

Here is a sample implementation:

```html
<!-- app.component.html -->
<div class="text-editor">
  <div>
    <!-- Text Area for normal text -->
    <textarea #normalText></textarea>

    <!-- List Items Container -->
    <ul [ngbMultiSelect]="listItems" (selectionChange)="onListSelection($event)">
      <li *ngFor="let item of selectedItems">{{ item.text }}</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .text-editor {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    width: 80%;
    margin: auto;
  }

  textarea {
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    padding: 5px;
    resize: none;
  }

  ul {
    height: 400px; /* Adjust this based on your needs */
    overflow-y: scroll;
  }

  li {
    padding: 5px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
</style>
```

4. **Component TypeScript File**:
   - Define the `listItems` array which will hold the different types of lists (unordered and ordered).
   - Implement the logic for handling list selection changes.

```typescript
// app.component.ts

import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { NgbMultiSelectModule } from '@ng-bootstrap/ng-bootstrap';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-text-editor',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css'],
  standalone: true,
  imports: [
    NgbMultiSelectModule
  ]
})
export class AppComponent {
  listItems = ['UL', 'OL'];
  selectedItems = [];

  constructor() {}

  onListSelection(event) {
    const { selectedOptions } = event;
    if (!selectedOptions.length) return;

    // Clear previous selections
    this.selectedItems.forEach(item => {
      this.listItems.indexOf(item) === selectedOptions[0].index && this.listItems.splice(item, 1);
    });

    selectedOptions.forEach(option => {
      option.value && this.listItems.push(option.value);
    });
  }

  addToList(item) {
    this.selectedItems.push({ text: item });
  }

  removeFromList(index) {
    this.selectedItems.splice(index, 1);
  }
}
```

5. **Explanation**:
   - The `ngbMultiSelect` directive is used to allow users to select multiple items from a list.
   - The `#normalText` variable references the `<textarea>` element where normal text can be entered.
   - The `ul` element wraps around the list items, and `[ngbMultiSelect]` attribute binds it to the `selectedItems` array.
   - The `(selectionChange)` event listener updates the `selectedItems` array whenever a change occurs in the multi-select dropdown.
   - The `addToList()` method allows adding individual list items directly, while the `removeFromList()` method removes them when needed.

